What is DispatcherServlet in Spring?

In Spring MVC, the DispatcherServlet is the front controller.

It sits at the center of the Spring web application and is responsible for handling all incoming HTTP requests and delegating them to the right controller.

Think of it as the traffic police that receives requests and decides which controller/method should process them, then returns the response.

Workflow of DispatcherServlet

Client Request

User sends a request (e.g., GET /users/1).

DispatcherServlet

The request first goes to the DispatcherServlet.

It is mapped in web.xml (in old XML config) or auto-configured by Spring Boot.

Handler Mapping

DispatcherServlet consults HandlerMapping to find the correct Controller/Method.

Controller Execution

The chosen controller method is called.

ViewResolver / Response Body

If it returns a view (e.g., JSP, Thymeleaf), ViewResolver resolves it.

If it’s a REST API (@RestController), it directly returns JSON/XML.

Response to Client

The final response is sent back to the browser.

Diagram (Simplified)
Client Request ---> DispatcherServlet ---> HandlerMapping ---> Controller ---> Service ---> DAO/DB
        |                                                               |
        <--------------------------- Response --------------------------+

In Spring Boot

DispatcherServlet is auto-registered.

Default URL mapping is "/" → meaning it handles all incoming requests.

You don’t need to configure it manually (unless customization is required).

Key Points

Core of Spring MVC (Front Controller Pattern).

Delegates requests to the right @Controller / @RestController.

Works with HandlerMapping, HandlerAdapter, and ViewResolver.

In REST APIs, it directly returns JSON/XML instead of a view.

⚡ So in short:
👉 DispatcherServlet = Request Dispatcher / Front Controller in Spring MVC that manages the whole request–response flow.




Great question 👍 In Spring Boot you rarely need to touch the DispatcherServlet directly because Boot configures it automatically. But if you do want to customize it, here are the ways:

1. Access and configure the existing DispatcherServlet

Spring Boot registers the DispatcherServlet as a bean named dispatcherServlet.
You can get hold of it and tweak its properties:

import org.springframework.boot.web.servlet.ServletRegistrationBean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.web.servlet.DispatcherServlet;

@Configuration
public class DispatcherConfig {

    @Bean
    public ServletRegistrationBean<DispatcherServlet> dispatcherServletRegistration(DispatcherServlet dispatcherServlet) {
        ServletRegistrationBean<DispatcherServlet> registration =
                new ServletRegistrationBean<>(dispatcherServlet, "/api/*"); // change mapping
        registration.setName("customDispatcherServlet");
        registration.setLoadOnStartup(1);
        return registration;
    }
}


👉 This lets you:

Change URL mapping (/api/* instead of /)

Add init parameters

Control startup order

2. Register multiple DispatcherServlets

You can even have multiple dispatchers handling different URL patterns:

@Bean
public ServletRegistrationBean<DispatcherServlet> adminDispatcherServlet() {
    DispatcherServlet dispatcherServlet = new DispatcherServlet();
    ServletRegistrationBean<DispatcherServlet> registration =
            new ServletRegistrationBean<>(dispatcherServlet, "/admin/*");
    registration.setName("adminServlet");
    return registration;
}


Here /admin/* requests are handled by a separate DispatcherServlet.

3. Customize DispatcherServlet properties

Spring Boot exposes DispatcherServlet properties in application.properties:

spring.mvc.dispatch-options-request=true
spring.mvc.throw-exception-if-no-handler-found=true
spring.mvc.servlet.load-on-startup=1

4. Programmatic customization with WebMvcConfigurer

If you just want to tweak handler mappings, message converters, interceptors, etc.,
you don’t need to touch the DispatcherServlet itself. Instead:

@Configuration
public class WebConfig implements WebMvcConfigurer {
    @Override
    public void addInterceptors(InterceptorRegistry registry) {
        registry.addInterceptor(new CustomInterceptor());
    }
}


✅ Summary:

Small tweaks → use application.properties or WebMvcConfigurer.

Custom URL mapping or multiple dispatchers → register your own ServletRegistrationBean.

Full custom DispatcherServlet → define a new @Bean DispatcherServlet.



